const intro={"n":32,"title":"Martín Fierro's Advice to His Sons: Brotherhood, Work and Nonviolence","nums":[1150,1160,1166,1167],"summary":"Fierro offers counsel to his two sons and Cruz's son before they part. Drawing on a lifetime of suffering, he values friendship, work, humility, solidarity among brothers, respect for elders, restraint and avoiding theft and unnecessary violence. He warns against drunkenness and speaks of responsibility in relationships. The advice contrasts with Old Vizcacha's more self-interested maxims from Canto XV, but Fierro is a complicated teacher: he previously killed men and attempts at times to justify those acts. His warning against killing is therefore best read alongside his own history.","people":"Martín Fierro as father and speaker; his two sons; Picardía, the son of Cruz; Old Vizcacha as the contrasting source of earlier advice, not a speaker here.","themes":"Brotherhood and loyalty; the ethics of work; respect and restraint; nonviolence and remorse; learning through suffering; contrast with pragmatic cynicism.","words":"estacada (in the expression dejar en la estacada, abandon someone in difficulty); picardía (cunning or trickery); vergüenza (honor or shame); ancianos (elders); vicio (vice); consejos (advice).","questions":["What does Fierro say about loyalty to a friend?","Why does he urge brothers to remain united?","How does the warning against killing relate to his own violent past?","How do his maxims about theft and work compare with Vizcacha's advice?","Which lessons can be read as universal ethical principles, and which require attention to the narrator's limitations?"],"analysis":"This is the explicit advice canto, not the preceding setup. Stanza 1160 gives the famous appeal to unity among brothers; stanzas 1166–1167 condemn theft and killing. The difference from Vizcacha is strong but must not be made absolute: both offer practical warnings, while Fierro speaks through an uneven personal history. Cross-references let readers compare exact Spanish stanzas rather than detached slogans.","related":"/en/library/old-vizcacha-vs-martin-fierro/","relatedTitle":"Old Vizcacha versus Martín Fierro: comparing advice"};
